WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.741
Mechanistic and organic describe broad approaches to
structure.

2
00:00:03.941 --> 00:00:09.990
Mechanistic features emphasize hierarchy, defined roles,
standardized work, and centralized decisions.

3
00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:16.110
Organic features emphasize flexible roles, lateral
communication, and more distributed decisions.

4
00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:18.751
Real organizations can combine both.

5
00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:23.481
Cedar Crossing benefits from a predictable confirmation
process for routine workshops.

6
00:00:23.681 --> 00:00:26.997
A new kind of event may also need specialists to adapt
together.

7
00:00:27.197 --> 00:00:35.854
Bureaucracy was intended to bring consistency, clear
responsibilities, and decisions based on roles and
competence rather than personal favoritism.

8
00:00:36.054 --> 00:00:40.512
Yet rules can become rigid, and departments can lose
sight of the people they serve.

9
00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:44.757
Removing every rule does not automatically create trust
or good service.

10
00:00:44.957 --> 00:00:47.849
Adding more rules does not guarantee fairness either.

11
00:00:48.049 --> 00:00:50.616
Ask which problem the structure is meant to address.

12
00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:55.974
Stable tasks, changing demands, staff preparation, and
coordination costs all matter.

13
00:00:56.174 --> 00:01:01.914
A flatter chart can speed some communication while
placing more demands on the people coordinating the
work.

